{"name":"24 Karat","hex":"#dfc685","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"karat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of carat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/karat"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fineness","blocks":[{"text":"The fineness of a precious metal object represents the weight of fine metal therein, in proportion to the total weight which includes alloying base metals and any impurities. Alloy metals are added to increase the hardness and durability of coins and jewelry, alter colors, decrease the cost per weight, or avoid the cost of high-purity refinement. For example, copper is added to the precious metal silver to make a more durable alloy for use in coins, housewares and jewelry. Coin silver, which was used for making silver coins in the past, contains 90% silver and 10% copper, by mass. Sterling silver contains 92.5% silver and 7.5% of other metals, usually copper, by mass."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fineness"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png/500px-2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png/1280px-2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png/500px-2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"2005 American Platinum Eagle noting .9995 platinum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2005_AEPlat_Proof_Rev.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Four_nines_fine_gold.jpeg/500px-Four_nines_fine_gold.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Four_nines_fine_gold.jpeg/500px-Four_nines_fine_gold.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1 troy ounce of four nines fine gold (999.9)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFour_nines_fine_gold.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Punc_Litva.png/250px-Punc_Litva.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Punc_Litva.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Punc_Litva.png/250px-Punc_Litva.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hallmarks can denote the millesimal fineness. These Lithuanian hallmarks are used for 18 karat gold and 14 karat gold.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APunc_Litva.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg/500px-2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg/1280px-2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg/500px-2019_American_Silver_Eagle_and_1943_Walking_Liberty_Half_dollar_side_by_side.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 2019 American Silver Eagle bullion coin with a fineness of 999 (three nines fine), together with a Walking Liberty half dollar wit